      "id": "0006",
      "front": "Find the coordinates of the minimum point of $y = x^2 - 4x + 5$.",
      "back": "(2, 1)",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "x coordinate is 2",
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "y coordinate is 1",
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "acceptedAliases": [
        "(2,1)"
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "(2, 1)",
        "distractors": [
          "(-2, 1)",
          "(2, -1)",
          "(4, 5)"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "Completing the square gives $y = (x - 2)^2 + 1$, so the minimum is $(2, 1)$. Equivalently the turning point of $ax^2 + bx + c$ has $x = -\\frac{b}{2a} = 2$. The answer $(-2, 1)$ takes the sign of $b$ the wrong way.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Paper 1: quadratics",
      "tagRefs": [
        "tmua",
        "paper-1"
      ]
    },
